$DIR/main.p:20:37: cannot convert f (variable of struct type Failure) to error: it contains shared[Detail], whose count is not atomic, and every error may cross tasks; use alloc.Synced (spec section 8.8)
    Every error value may be sent to another task, so a value can become an
    error (or an interface that embeds error) only if its type is sendable. This
    type holds a shared[T], whose reference count is changed with ordinary
    arithmetic, so two tasks copying or dropping references to it at once would
    corrupt it.
    The message names the shared[T]. alloc.Synced allocates an object whose
    references are counted atomically.
    Go's garbage collector makes every error safe to share. Plutonium counts a
    shared reference without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one
    task, and it counts the references to an error object in the object,
    atomically.
    Hold the data in a synced[T], which counts atomically:
        type Failure struct {
            detail synced[Detail]
        }
    (see "Sendable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.8)
    (run "pluto explain E2020" for a longer explanation)

